<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollonius of Tyana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 15–c. 100 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyana">Tyana</a> was a Greek Neopythagorean philosopher and wandering teacher from Cappadocia in the 1st century CE. His life is known chiefly from a biography by Philostratus, written over a century later, which credits him with miracles and travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> under a Parthian king. Later pagans presented him as a rival to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-tyana.jpg" alt="Apollonius of Tyana" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philosopher,_probably_Apollonius_of_Tyana,_marble,_ca_200_AD,_from_Gortys,_AMH,_145418.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings cultivated Greek opinion: Leucon held Athenian citizenship and tax privileges, and an Athenian decree of 347/346 BCE honoured his sons Spairocus, Paerisades and <b>Apollonius</b> with gold crowns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The apocryphal Acts of Thomas tell how the Apostle Thomas came to the court of King Gudnaphar, a name matching Gondophares; the Greek sage <b>Apollonius</b> reportedly visited Taxila under a Parthian king.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
